 -1.2381 TD0.0021 Tc-0.05 Tw[(each client request to be isolated from every other request. T)-31.4(ransactions enable)]TJT*0.0039 Tc-0.0321 Tw(clients to )Tj/F13 1 Tf4.2857 0 TD(believe)Tj/F11 1 Tf3.0476 0 TD[( they are dealing with a single in-memory bean instance, when)]TJ-7.3333 -1.2381 TD0.0452 Tw(in fact there are many instances behind the scenes. They give clients the illu-)TjT*-0.0455 Tw(sion that they have exclusive access to data when in fact there are many clients)TjT*0.0006 Tw(all touching the same data.)Tj/F7 1 Tf13.5 0 0 13.5 76 314 Tm0 Tc0.0003 Tw(Entity Bean Instances Can Be Pooled)Tj/F11 1 Tf10.5 0 0 10.5 112 293 Tm0.0039 Tc0.0303 Tw[(Let\325)7.6(s say you\325ve decided to author your own EJB container/server)17.5(. Y)105.5(our prod-)]TJT*0.0043 Tc0.0626 Tw[(uct is responsible for instantiating entity beans as necessary)41.8(, with each bean)]TJT*0.0039 Tc-0.0079 Tw[(representing data in an underlying storage. As clients connect and disconnect,)]TJT*-0.0001 Tw(you could create and destroy beans as necessary to service those clients.)Tj0 -1.8095 TD0.01 Tc0.0625 Tw[(Unfortunately)51.4(, this is not a very scalable way to build an application server)18.2(.)]TJ0 -1.2381 TD-0.0081 Tc-0.0499 Tw[(Creation and destruction of objects is very expensive, especially if client requests)]TJT*0.0039 Tc0.0002 Tw(come frequently. How can we save on this overhead?)Tj0 -1.9048 TD0.0012 Tc-0.05 Tw(One thing to remember is that an entity bean class describes the fields and rules)Tj0 -1.2381 TD0.0039 Tc0.0078 Tw[(for your entity bean, but it does not dictate any specific data. For example, an)]TJT*0 Tw(entity bean class may specify that all bank accounts have the following fields:)Tj/F20 1 Tf6 0 0 6 112 145 Tm0 Tc(n)Tj0.5 0 TD(n)Tj/F11 1 Tf10.5 0 0 10.5 127 144 Tm0.0039 Tc-0.0003 Tw(The name of the bank account owner)Tj/F20 1 Tf6 0 0 6 112 128 Tm0 Tc0 Tw(n)Tj0.5 0 TD(n)Tj/F11 1 Tf10.5 0 