-0.0166 Tw[(\(for example, JDBC traffic mixed with JNDI traffic\) all on one socket. This op-)]TJT*0.0005 Tw(timization leads to enhanced performance.)Tj0 -1.9048 TD0.0109 Tc0.0625 Tw[(More importantly)67(, W)55.7(ebLogic\325)40.7(s implementation of the JNDI specification is a)]TJ0 -1.2381 TD0.0039 Tc0.0099 Tw[(distributed implementation of JNDI. It is distributed in the sense that its JNDI)]TJT*-0.034 Tw(implementation can )Tj/F13 1 Tf8.9524 0 TD(delegate)Tj/F11 1 Tf3.619 0 TD[( to a JNDI service provider that is located on a dif-)]TJ-12.5714 -1.2381 TD-0.0264 Tw[(ferent machine across a network. This means the W)-6.7(ebLogic service provider is)]TJT*0.0001 Tc-0.05 Tw[(acting as a proxy for the real service provider)11.6(, such as an LDAP service provider)]TJT*0.0039 Tc-0.0381 Tw[(or an NDS service provider)-24.3(. This distributed JNDI implementation is especially)]TJT*-0.0008 Tc-0.05 Tw[(useful if your JNDI client is a Java applet. Java applets have security restrictions)]TJT*0.0039 Tc0.0233 Tw[(that prevent them from running native code \(unless they are digitally signed\).)]TJT*-0.023 Tw[(And unfortunately)18.4(, JNDI service providers often contain native code, such as a)]TJT*0.0248 Tw[(service provider that may access the W)19.9(indows NT registry)72.9(. Because JNDI ser)-15.3(-)]TJT*0.01 Tw[(vice providers may contain native code, they cannot run within the context of)]TJT*-0.0019 Tw[(an unsigned Java applet. WebLogic\325)80.2(s delegation method allows service provid-)]TJT*-0.0229 Tw(ers to be located on a secure machine and still be usable by clients that are not)TjT*(secure.)Tj0 -1.8095 TD-0.0309 Tw[(Because BEA has actually shipped a complete JNDI implementation, it is more)]TJ0 -1.2381 TD-0.001 Tc-0.0499 Tw[(than just a service provider)45(. Note that this is merely an example of a JNDI imple-)]TJT*0.0039 Tc-0.0001 Tw(mentation; the playing ground is open for other vendors to do the same.)TjET0.8 0.8 0.8 RG24 w 10 M /GS1 gs67 111 m97 111 lS0 0 0 RG1 w 67 99.5 m464 99.5 lSBT/F7 1 Tf15 0 0 15 73 105 Tm/GS2 gs0 Tc-0.0002 Tw[(Understanding the Concepts behind J)-58.7(N)-4.3(DI Programming)]TJ/F11 1 Tf10.5 0 0 10.5 103 77 Tm0.0049 Tc0.0625 Tw[(Before we embark on a JNDI example, let\325)31.3(s lay a foundation of the concepts)]TJT*0.0039 Tc-0.0001 Tw[(behind JNDI. W)-35.9(e)0( begin with naming concepts.)]TJETendstreamendobj1902 0 obj<</ProcSet [/PDF /Text ]/Font <</F7 8 0 R/F11 10 0 R/F13 18 0 R/F15 22 0 R/F17 29 0 R>>/ExtGState <</GS1 11 0 R/GS2 12 0 R>>/ColorSpace <</CS2 30 0 R>>>>endobj1904 0 obj<</Length 5744>>stream
